The table structure information is (table names): 
['player', 'team', 'match', 'player_match', 'ball_by_ball', 'batsman_scored', 'wicket_taken', 'extra_runs']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used 

player  
• player_id : joins to every other table to attach a human‑readable name  
• player_name : final presentation of the bowler’s name  

ball_by_ball  
• match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no : composite key used to join to batsman_scored, wicket_taken, extra_runs  
• bowler : the bowler who delivered the ball – basis for every bowling calculation  
• team_bowling : may be handy when we want to know the bowling side  

batsman_scored  
• match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no : join columns to ball_by_ball  
• runs_scored : runs off the bat; will be SUM‑med to obtain “runs conceded” (economy & best figures)  

extra_runs  
• match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no : join columns  
• extra_type : to filter out wides / no‑balls (balls that should not count towards the legal‑ball / over count, and whose runs we must ignore)  
• extra_runs : ignored in the economy‑rate numerator because the task says “ignore any extra runs like wides and no‑balls”  

wicket_taken  
• match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no : join columns  
• kind_out : we will discard (‘run out’, ‘retired hurt’, ‘obstructing the field’ … – anything in which the bowler does not get credit)  
• player_out : not needed for this task, but sometimes useful for checks  

Typical conditions that will appear  
• WHERE extra_type IS NULL OR extra_type NOT IN ('wides','noballs','no ball','wide')  – keep only legal deliveries  
• WHERE kind_out NOT IN ('run out','retired hurt','obstructing the field','hit wicket (non‑bowler)') – keep only bowler wickets  

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Below are 10 exploratory SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that progressively look at the columns we are going to use.  
Every query is wrapped in ```sql``` and preceded by a short comment.

```sql
-- 1) Distinct dismissal types to see which ones must be excluded from “bowler wickets”
SELECT DISTINCT kind_out
FROM wicket_taken
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Distinct extra types to spot wides / no‑balls etc.
SELECT DISTINCT extra_type
FROM extra_runs
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Quick look at how many runs are recorded off the bat for a few deliveries
SELECT match_id, over_id, ball_id, runs_scored
FROM batsman_scored
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Join wicket_taken to ball_by_ball to confirm we can retrieve the bowler who took each wicket
SELECT wt.match_id,
       wt.over_id,
       wt.ball_id,
       wt.kind_out,
       bb.bowler
FROM   wicket_taken AS wt
JOIN   ball_by_ball AS bb
  ON   wt.match_id = bb.match_id
 AND   wt.over_id  = bb.over_id
 AND   wt.ball_id  = bb.ball_id
 AND   wt.innings_no = bb.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Example of a legal delivery (no wides / no‑balls) with its runs off bat
SELECT bb.match_id,
       bb.over_id,
       bb.ball_id,
       bb.bowler,
       bs.runs_scored
FROM   ball_by_ball AS bb
JOIN   batsman_scored AS bs
  ON   bb.match_id = bs.match_id
 AND   bb.over_id  = bs.over_id
 AND   bb.ball_id  = bs.ball_id
 AND   bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
LEFT  JOIN extra_runs AS er          -- filter out balls that are wides / no‑balls
  ON   bb.match_id = er.match_id
 AND   bb.over_id  = er.over_id
 AND   bb.ball_id  = er.ball_id
 AND   bb.innings_no = er.innings_no
WHERE  er.extra_type IS NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Total bowler wickets (excluding run‑outs etc.) – quick aggregation preview
SELECT p.player_name,
       COUNT(*) AS wickets
FROM   wicket_taken AS wt
JOIN   ball_by_ball AS bb
  ON   wt.match_id   = bb.match_id
 AND   wt.over_id    = bb.over_id
 AND   wt.ball_id    = bb.ball_id
 AND   wt.innings_no = bb.innings_no
JOIN   player AS p
  ON   bb.bowler = p.player_id
WHERE  wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out','retired hurt','obstructing the field')
GROUP  BY p.player_name
ORDER  BY wickets DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Runs conceded off the bat by every bowler (ignoring extras) – preview
SELECT p.player_name,
       SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS runs_conceded
FROM   ball_by_ball  AS bb
JOIN   batsman_scored AS bs
  ON   bb.match_id   = bs.match_id
 AND   bb.over_id    = bs.over_id
 AND   bb.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
 AND   bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
LEFT  JOIN extra_runs AS er
  ON   bb.match_id   = er.match_id
 AND   bb.over_id    = er.over_id
 AND   bb.ball_id    = er.ball_id
 AND   bb.innings_no = er.innings_no
JOIN   player AS p
  ON   bb.bowler     = p.player_id
WHERE  er.extra_type IS NULL      -- keep legal deliveries only
GROUP  BY p.player_name
ORDER  BY runs_conceded DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Ball count and over count (legal balls only) bowled by one sample bowler – shows calculation base for economy / strike rate
SELECT p.player_name,
       COUNT(*)                AS legal_balls,
       COUNT(*) / 6.0          AS overs_bowled
FROM   ball_by_ball  AS bb
LEFT  JOIN extra_runs AS er
  ON   bb.match_id   = er.match_id
 AND   bb.over_id    = er.over_id
 AND   bb.ball_id    = er.ball_id
 AND   bb.innings_no = er.innings_no
JOIN   player AS p
  ON   bb.bowler     = p.player_id
WHERE  p.player_name = 'SC Ganguly'       -- sample bowler; replace as needed
  AND (er.extra_type IS NULL OR er.extra_type NOT IN ('wides','noballs','wide','no ball'))
GROUP  BY p.player_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Best bowling figures (most wickets, lowest runs for that wicket count) for a bowler across matches
SELECT p.player_name,
       bb.match_id,
       COUNT(*)                             AS wickets_in_match,
       SUM(bs.runs_scored)                  AS runs_off_bat
FROM   wicket_taken AS wt
JOIN   ball_by_ball AS bb
  ON   wt.match_id   = bb.match_id
 AND   wt.over_id    = bb.over_id
 AND   wt.ball_id    = bb.ball_id
 AND   wt.innings_no = bb.innings_no
JOIN   batsman_scored AS bs
  ON   bb.match_id   = bs.match_id
 AND   bb.over_id    = bs.over_id
 AND   bb.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
 AND   bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
LEFT  JOIN extra_runs AS er
  ON   bb.match_id   = er.match_id
 AND   bb.over_id    = er.over_id
 AND   bb.ball_id    = er.ball_id
 AND   bb.innings_no = er.innings_no
JOIN   player AS p
  ON   bb.bowler     = p.player_id
WHERE  wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out','retired hurt','obstructing the field')
  AND (er.extra_type IS NULL OR er.extra_type NOT IN ('wides','noballs','wide','no ball'))
  AND p.player_name = 'SC Ganguly'          -- sample bowler
GROUP  BY p.player_name, bb.match_id
ORDER  BY wickets_in_match DESC,
          runs_off_bat ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Putting it all together: comprehensive metrics (wickets, economy, strike rate, best‑figures) for every bowler
WITH legal_balls AS (
    SELECT bb.match_id,
           bb.over_id,
           bb.ball_id,
           bb.innings_no,
           bb.bowler,
           bs.runs_scored
    FROM   ball_by_ball AS bb
    JOIN   batsman_scored AS bs
      ON   bb.match_id   = bs.match_id
     AND   bb.over_id    = bs.over_id
     AND   bb.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
     AND   bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
    LEFT  JOIN extra_runs AS er
      ON   bb.match_id   = er.match_id
     AND   bb.over_id    = er.over_id
     AND   bb.ball_id    = er.ball_id
     AND   bb.innings_no = er.innings_no
    WHERE  er.extra_type IS NULL
           OR er.extra_type NOT IN ('wides','noballs','wide','no ball')
),
bowler_wickets AS (
    SELECT bb.bowler,
           COUNT(*) AS wkts
    FROM   wicket_taken AS wt
    JOIN   ball_by_ball AS bb
      ON   wt.match_id   = bb.match_id
     AND   wt.over_id    = bb.over_id
     AND   wt.ball_id    = bb.ball_id
     AND   wt.innings_no = bb.innings_no
    WHERE  wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out','retired hurt','obstructing the field')
    GROUP  BY bb.bowler
),
best_match AS (
    SELECT bb.bowler,
           bb.match_id,
           COUNT(*)                 AS wkts_in_match,
           SUM(bs.runs_scored)      AS runs_in_match,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY bb.bowler
                              ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, SUM(bs.runs_scored) ASC) AS rn
    FROM   wicket_taken AS wt
    JOIN   ball_by_ball AS bb
      ON   wt.match_id   = bb.match_id
     AND   wt.over_id    = bb.over_id
     AND   wt.ball_id    = bb.ball_id
     AND   wt.innings_no = bb.innings_no
    JOIN   legal_balls   AS bs
      ON   bb.match_id   = bs.match_id
     AND   bb.over_id    = bs.over_id
     AND   bb.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
     AND   bb.innings_no = bs.innings_no
    GROUP  BY bb.bowler, bb.match_id
)
SELECT p.player_name                           AS bowler,
       bw.wkts                                 AS total_wkts,
       ROUND(SUM(lb.runs_scored)*1.0 / (COUNT(*)/6.0), 2) AS economy_rate,
       ROUND(COUNT(*)*1.0 / bw.wkts, 2)        AS strike_rate,
       printf('%d-%d', bm.wkts_in_match, bm.runs_in_match) AS best_figures
FROM   legal_balls AS lb
JOIN   bowler_wickets AS bw
  ON   lb.bowler = bw.bowler
LEFT  JOIN best_match AS bm
  ON   lb.bowler = bm.bowler
 AND   bm.rn = 1
JOIN   player AS p
  ON   lb.bowler = p.player_id
GROUP  BY lb.bowler
ORDER  BY total_wkts DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

These 10 queries incrementally inspect the data and culminate in a single statement that produces, for every bowler,  
• total wickets (counting only bowler‑credited dismissals)  
• economy rate (runs off the bat ÷ overs bowled where overs = legal balls / 6)  
• strike rate (legal balls per wicket)  
• best bowling in a match, formatted as “wickets‑runs”.
